# Why does val CE stay at ~0.62 here when the old lambda-scan runs reached 0.599-0.600 on
# 112022 (rriva, 2026-10-28)? Leading explanation: labelled data, 18.24M distinct jets here
# vs 51.16M in 112022 train (experiments/2026-10_ssl-campaign/diagnostics/dup_check.py; duplicates ~2%, no split overlap).
# Other candidate: the batch, 28672 here vs 2048 in the old recipe.
#   sc_112022  the S3 SC winner on the 112022 data, scored on its own val: ~0.600 means the
#              data explains the gap. 112022 train shares ~43% of its jets with every 152051
#              split, so compare it only with the old runs on 112022 val.
#   sc_bs2048  the old recipe's batch 2048 and lr 3e-4 on 152051. Chunk 256, as the batch must
#              be a multiple of 8 x chunk (S1: chunks 128 and 512 give the same CE).
#
#   ENCODERS=rowcol_concat_v0 CHUNK=512 SEEDS=42 PACKS=1 MEM_PER_GPU_GB=32 MAX_EPOCHS=40 \
#   EST_SECONDS_PER_EPOCH=3500 WALLTIME=2-00:00:00 \
#   CELLS=experiments/2026-10_ssl-campaign/configs/C_gap.cells bash slurm/ssl_pack.sh
sc_112022  arm=scratch encoders=${ENCODERS} chunk_size=${CHUNK} base_path=/storage3/DSIP/rriva/datasets/transforming-jet-flavor/data_parallel_preprocess_251112_112022 datamodule.batch_size=28672 optimizer.lr=1.1e-3 @est=3500
sc_bs2048  arm=scratch encoders=${ENCODERS} chunk_size=256 datamodule.batch_size=2048 optimizer.lr=3e-4 @est=3000
